  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x} & =\\frac{5(2)+3}{4x}=\\frac{13}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n4x=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=0.$",
